[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says every report in it is one bug: the parser reads dates month
first. That holds for most of them, not all. Read by cause instead of by subject line, the
ticket holds three bugs, and each now has a ticket of its own.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser closes only the first ticket. The other two keep coming in after the fix.
</Callout>

## The three bugs

**`parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first.** Every tenant in #4471 writes
the day first. Any date with a day of twelve or less comes out with day and month swapped: 7 April
becomes 4 July. Reports from the app, from email and from the booking-v2 form land here.

**`hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type month first.** booking-v3 prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says, and the form reads the date the
tenant's way. The input is already wrong, so a fixed parser still flips it. Only reports through
the booking-v3 form, from the tenants that run it.

**`shift`: dates move by one day.** A swap moves a date by months. These reports say "Tuesday 12
May" became "Monday 11 May", "every booking in June is one day off", and "Client in Lisbon got
Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." That is a time zone conversion, not the parser. The desk
had already read them as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`); now they count as their own cause.

## How they were counted

- The export lists some reports twice. One id, one report.
- The cause comes from the report's tenant, its booking form and its channel (`causes.ts`), not
  from the intake tag. Intake guessed from the reporter's browser; the column "tagged as" shows
  how often it guessed the other bug.
- `shift` comes first: a report the desk read as a one-day move is `shift` whatever its tenant.

## Lessons

- Matching on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) merged three
  bugs into one ticket. Read the bodies before you merge.
- "Known" is a claim about one cause. Check it against the table before you close the queue.

